    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4ad46e82-5227-557b-9136-f3a4362e0bfc",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/2dc97da81abb7b1d52a516511ba95c25f1df5cfc2b3f9f3e9be9a8d1b62d35c3.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=2x-1$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-2,-2)$, $B(-1,0)$, $C(-3,-7)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-2,-2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x-1 \\\\ (-2) &= 2(-2)-1 \\\\ -2 &= -5 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-1,0)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x-1 \\\\ (0) &= 2(-1)-1 \\\\ 0 &= -3 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-3,-7)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x-1 \\\\ (-7) &= 2(-3)-1 \\\\ -7 &= -7 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $C$ lies on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "f7b65589-3c4b-5e96-aabd-97d85e89747f",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
