    "question": {
      ":question/id": "47697d13-3924-5873-a0d2-f29c8f7b3c97",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/cd54daeaca37cd928159e74f6ecb23940bc5570ffff665fd674f0b047d8147bd.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=2x$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(3,5)$, $B(-4,-8)$, $C(2,4)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(3,5)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x \\\\ (5) &= 2(3) \\\\ 5 &= 6 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-4,-8)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x \\\\ (-8) &= 2(-4) \\\\ -8 &= -8 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x \\\\ (4) &= 2(2) \\\\ 4 &= 4 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $B$ and $C$ lie on the line.",
