      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/6b2821c46786f28cc1ce29b6ab5d2d2cc186b4c5991490ea077837a7ecc3ebbc.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=2x+2$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-1,0)$, $B(-4,-8)$, $C(1,4)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-1,0)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x+2 \\\\ (0) &= 2(-1)+2 \\\\ 0 &= 0 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-4,-8)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x+2 \\\\ (-8) &= 2(-4)+2 \\\\ -8 &= -6 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(1,4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x+2 \\\\ (4) &= 2(1)+2 \\\\ 4 &= 4 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line.",
