    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c26030cf-1d74-56ce-b13c-d5fa5616c334",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/6c5fbeec5735452d2cffecb6fca7c2594b24177b9f6c65fb5ff4fd98f454bd0f.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=2x+1$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-3,-3)$, $B(3,7)$, $C(-2,-1)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-3,-3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x+1 \\\\ (-3) &= 2(-3)+1 \\\\ -3 &= -5 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(3,7)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x+1 \\\\ (7) &= 2(3)+1 \\\\ 7 &= 7 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-2,-1)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x+1 \\\\ (-1) &= 2(-2)+1 \\\\ -1 &= -3 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $B$ lies on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "95a908f0-dc51-5d84-a03b-33853897fd24",
