      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/72afb81c6f8261afbbc6992cdc65c9fe0b214fadb9801647caf865bf17e09d19.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=x+2$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-2,-3)$, $B(-4,0)$, $C(-3,-1)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-2,-3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= x+2 \\\\ (-3) &= (-2)+2 \\\\ -3 &= 0 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-4,0)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= x+2 \\\\ (0) &= (-4)+2 \\\\ 0 &= -2 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-3,-1)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= x+2 \\\\ (-1) &= (-3)+2 \\\\ -1 &= -1 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $C$ lies on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
