  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186054676,
      "id": "3123f8ed-a1ca-52b7-8427-2f3b60d9e68a",
      "title": "Identifying Points That Lie on a Line"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "9b93a8e5-ff0d-5678-8f35-b11df8418cae",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/2bb8392132b993578158051b627dd7b04977803d427ba8537d51cfe9e50fbef8.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=2x$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(3,6)$, $B(-2,-2)$, $C(1,2)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(3,6)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x \\\\ (6) &= 2(3) \\\\ 6 &= 6 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-2,-2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x \\\\ (-2) &= 2(-2) \\\\ -2 &= -4 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(1,2)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x \\\\ (2) &= 2(1) \\\\ 2 &= 2 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "2e349b10-60e3-5dc0-a72c-eedacfa8d217",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
