    "question": {
      ":question/id": "9d675bc3-3a84-58d7-bbb4-e2f72f4aca2f",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/1e742b2f4085f6bcfd4db0d92881a688e68bd1dccaf5a5bab75b75ca88de80a3.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=x-1$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-4,-5)$, $B(2,-2)$, $C(-2,-2)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-4,-5)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= x-1 \\\\ (-5) &= (-4)-1 \\\\ -5 &= -5 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(2,-2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= x-1 \\\\ (-2) &= (2)-1 \\\\ -2 &= 1 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-2,-2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= x-1 \\\\ (-2) &= (-2)-1 \\\\ -2 &= -3 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $A$ lies on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "9368ee3d-6ef2-5564-9d6f-710f5fa6997d",
