    "question": {
      ":question/id": "60106560-bc0b-5fd5-89e3-18c93e1d6951",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/2892ffba3c1c22d260920bd34bcf4b05e5d1671e99af55cc61f09ad9aa2d1899.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=2x+1$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-2,-3)$, $B(0,0)$, $C(-1,1)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-2,-3)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x+1 \\\\ (-3) &= 2(-2)+1 \\\\ -3 &= -3 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(0,0)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x+1 \\\\ (0) &= 2(0)+1 \\\\ 0 &= 1 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-1,1)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x+1 \\\\ (1) &= 2(-1)+1 \\\\ 1 &= -1 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $A$ lies on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "1a1e3bce-89f9-50c4-8daf-fbd057bf17e5",
