    "question": {
      ":question/id": "da8c8e27-06ee-50c1-b2a2-0f685b36b16a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/b86ff4d254e33b2a0fb47d26884c4c910bfaa282779fcaf42d28c1807efa5683.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=x-1$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,-1)$, $B(-1,-3)$, $C(-3,-5)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,-1)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= x-1 \\\\ (-1) &= (0)-1 \\\\ -1 &= -1 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-1,-3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= x-1 \\\\ (-3) &= (-1)-1 \\\\ -3 &= -2 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-3,-5)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= x-1 \\\\ (-5) &= (-3)-1 \\\\ -5 &= -4 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $A$ lies on the line.",
